---
id: org.dxos.echo-sqlite
name: EchoSqlite
version: 0.1.0
---

`@dxos/echo-sqlite` is the smallest backend that implements the ECHO `Database` interface from
`@dxos/echo` without `@dxos/echo-client` / `@dxos/echo-host`: no Automerge, no feeds, no replication,
no client/host split. Entities are stored as JSON rows in SQLite and surface to callers as ordinary
in-memory live objects (`Obj.make` proxies).

Two properties define it and outrank simplicity:

- **SQLite is the source of truth and the space is never resident.** Opening a database, querying it,
  or resolving a ref materializes only the rows that call needs. Memory is bounded by what callers hold,
  not by the size of the space [F-3].
- **Queries run in SQLite.** A query AST compiles to one read-only SQL statement, following the design
  in `packages/core/echo/echo-host/docs/SQL_QUERY_EXECUTION.md` and its compiler
  `packages/core/echo/echo-host/src/query/sql/compile.ts`. Only the result rows are hydrated; nothing is
  filtered, traversed or ordered in JavaScript [F-4].

The package started as a whole-space-in-memory prototype (loads every row on open, evaluates queries by
scanning the working set). That prototype does not meet [F-3] or [F-4]; this spec is the target it is
rewritten to.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.

| Term       | URI                         |
| ---------- | --------------------------- |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`     |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0` |
| `rule`     | `org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0`     |

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Database interface

  req F-1.1:
    when: SqliteDatabase is constructed
    then: it satisfies Database.Database, Database.isDatabase(db) is true, and db.graph is a
          Hypergraph whose getDatabase(db.spaceId) returns db

  req F-1.2:
    when: the database is opened with an Effect SqlClient in scope
    then: it runs on every SqlClient the repo supports — node (@effect/sql-sqlite-node), browser
          (wa-sqlite over OPFS via @dxos/sql-sqlite) and bun — with no platform-specific code in the
          package

  req F-1.3:
    when: code runs in Effect
    then: SqliteDatabase.layer(options) provides Database.Service, and closing its scope flushes
          pending writes and releases every subscription

  req F-1.4:
    when: a caller uses feeds, branches, history (getVersion) or a non-inline blob backend
    then: the call fails with UnsupportedOperationError; nothing silently no-ops or returns a
          fabricated value
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Storage layout

  req F-2.1:
    when: an entity is stored
    then: it is one row of the entity table, keyed by (spaceId, id), holding the full ECHO JSON
          (Obj.toJSON plus @parent, @meta included) as the body

  req F-2.2:
    when: the compiler needs a field to select, join or order on
    then: that field is a denormalized, indexed column written in the same transaction as the body —
          kind, typeDXN, deleted, createdAt, updatedAt, the raw parent/source/target URIs, and the
          normalized parentId/sourceId/targetId (the bare entity id when local to the row's space,
          NULL otherwise)

  req F-2.3:
    when: an entity holding references or text is written
    then: its outgoing references (target id, normalized property path) and its extracted string
          values are written to a reverse-reference side table and an FTS5 (trigram) side table in
          the same transaction, so incoming-reference and text queries never read bodies

  req F-2.4:
    when: the schema changes
    then: the change is a new numbered, immutable .sql migration in src/migrations with its own
          history table, applied through Migrator.make({}); every CREATE carries IF NOT EXISTS
          (see .agents/projects/sql-migrations/DESIGN.md)

  req F-2.5:
    when: several spaces share one SQLite file
    then: every statement is constrained to its spaceId and no space can read or write another's rows
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Bounded memory — the space is never loaded whole

  desc: |
    The non-negotiable requirement. Nothing in the package may read every row of a space into memory
    as part of normal operation: not on open, not to answer a query, not to resolve a ref, not to
    notify a subscriber. Memory must scale with what callers hold, not with the size of the space.

  req F-3.1:
    when: a database is opened
    then: it runs migrations and reads at most the persisted type rows (kind = type); no object or
          relation row is read or hydrated

  req F-3.2:
    when: a query runs
    then: only the rows in its final result are read from SQLite and hydrated; a LIMIT bounds the
          rows read, not just the rows returned

  req F-3.3:
    when: a ref is loaded (ref.load(), Database.load, db.makeRef(uri).load())
    then: the single target row is read by (spaceId, id) and hydrated; ref.target / ref.peek()
          answer synchronously from the working set only and never trigger a scan

  req F-3.4:
    when: a row is hydrated
    then: it enters a working set keyed by id; while any caller still holds that live object, every
          lookup of the same id (query result, ref, getObjectById) returns the same instance

  req F-3.5:
    when: no caller holds a hydrated object any longer and it has no unflushed changes
    then: the working set releases it (weakly held); the working set never pins objects for the
          lifetime of the database

  req F-3.6:
    when: relations, parents or references are added or resolved
    then: endpoints and targets are resolved one row at a time on demand; no traversal preloads a
          subgraph
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Queries compile to SQL

  desc: |
    Follows packages/core/echo/echo-host/docs/SQL_QUERY_EXECUTION.md. The compiler may be shared with
    echo-host's SqlPlanCompiler where the table layout allows, or ported; either way the semantics
    and invariants below match it.

  req F-4.1:
    when: a query (Query or Filter) is executed
    then: it compiles to exactly one read-only statement, WITH RECURSIVE with one CTE per plan step
          and a final SELECT; sub-plans (in-query, union branches, set-difference) compile into the
          same statement

  req F-4.2:
    when: the compiler builds SQL
    then: compilation is pure (plan in, SQL out, no store reads); every value is a bound parameter,
          lists are bound as one JSON array read with json_each(?), and only JSON paths and
          identifiers are emitted as literals

  req F-4.3:
    when: a property filter is compiled
    then: it reads json_extract(body, '$."k1"."k2"') with typed comparison — gt/gte/lt/lte match
          only when operand and value share a JSON type, eq/neq/in/contains/range/eq-undefined and
          nested object filters follow filterMatchEntity, and every type test uses
          COALESCE(json_type(...), 'missing') so NOT keeps three-valued logic correct

  req F-4.4:
    when: a query selects by type, id, timestamp, tag, foreign key, meta key or text
    then: type/id/timestamp read the denormalized columns through their indexes, tag/foreignKeys/
          metaKey read $."@meta" in the body, and text search reads the FTS5 table ranked by bm25

  req F-4.5:
    when: a query traverses references, incoming references, relations or the hierarchy
    then: the traversal is a join on the normalized id columns or the reverse-reference table, and
          results are de-duplicated by row

  req F-4.6:
    when: deleted objects are in scope
    then: an object is deleted if its own row is deleted or any parent (up to depth 10) or relation
          endpoint is; options({ deleted }) selects exclude (default) / include / only in SQL

  req F-4.7:
    when: a query orders, limits or skips
    then: order, LIMIT and OFFSET run in SQL; natural order is by id in BINARY (code-unit)
          collation, NULLS LAST, with id as the final tiebreaker, so results are deterministic

  req F-4.8:
    when: a clause cannot be expressed as SQL without first reading the store (e.g. a metaVersion
          semver range)
    then: it is resolved by a pre-pass statement that turns it into literals; if no pre-pass exists
          the query fails with UnsupportedQueryError — there is no fallback that scans or filters
          the space in JavaScript

  req F-4.9:
    when: a query runs while the database has unflushed changes
    then: pending writes are committed before the statement executes, so run() always reflects
          every mutation made before it was called (read-your-writes)

  req F-4.10:
    when: a query is executed against the registry scope (Scope.registry())
    then: it is answered from the in-process registry, which is small and code-shipped, and is the
          one place in-memory matching is allowed
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Query results and reactivity

  req F-5.1:
    when: QueryResult.run() / runEntries() / first() is called
    then: the compiled statement executes and the hydrated results are returned

  req F-5.2:
    when: QueryResult.runSync() / .results / .entries is read
    then: it returns the results of the most recent execution of that query (empty before the first
          one) and schedules an execution if none is current; it never executes SQL synchronously and
          never scans the working set

  req F-5.3:
    when: a query has a subscriber (subscribe or atom)
    then: it executes once on subscribe and re-executes after each committed write batch that could
          affect it — any write when the query is not simple, otherwise a write whose typenames, ids
          or spaces overlap the query's scope

  req F-5.4:
    when: a re-execution returns the same rows in the same order
    then: subscribers are not notified; they are notified only when membership or order changes
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Writes and durability

  req F-6.1:
    when: an entity is added, mutated (Obj.update, nested or meta changes) or removed
    then: only that entity's row (and its side-table rows) is rewritten, batched per microtask into
          one transaction; db.flush() resolves once the batch is durable

  req F-6.2:
    when: an object added to the database references (Ref.make) or relates to entities not yet stored
    then: those entities are stored with it in the same batch

  req F-6.3:
    when: db.remove(obj) is called
    then: the row is soft-deleted (deleted = 1), re-adding the object restores it, and
          runGarbageCollection hard-deletes soft-deleted rows and their side-table rows

  req F-6.4:
    when: a write batch fails
    then: the batch stays pending and the next flush retries it and rejects if it fails again;
          memory and disk never silently diverge, and close() releases subscriptions even when its
          final flush fails

  req F-6.5:
    when: the process restarts and the database is reopened
    then: every flushed entity — body, meta, parent, relation endpoints, references, deletion state,
          timestamps — reads back identically
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Types

  req F-7.1:
    when: static types are passed to open({ types })
    then: rows of those types hydrate as typed live objects

  req F-7.2:
    when: db.addType(type) is called
    then: a Type entity with the type's JSON Schema is stored (idempotent on typename + version) and
          registered, so its objects hydrate after reopen without code-side registration

  req F-7.3:
    when: a row's type is unknown
    then: it hydrates as a read-only snapshot instead of failing the query that returned it
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: Opening a large space reads nothing
  given: a file holding a space of 100,000 objects
  when: the database is opened and one query with limit(10) runs
  then:
    - open reads zero object rows
    - the query hydrates exactly 10 objects
    - heap growth stays bounded independent of the space size
  tags: [memory, F-3]
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: The working set releases unheld objects
  given: 10,000 objects hydrated by queries whose results are then dropped
  when: garbage collection runs (node --expose-gc)
  then:
    - the working set size returns to the number of objects still referenced
    - a later lookup of a released id re-reads its row and returns a fresh live object
  tags: [memory, F-3]
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: Differential against the in-memory matcher
  given: a seeded generator of objects with mixed-type fields, nested objects, arrays and refs
  when: 200 random filters (eq/neq/gt/lt/in/range/contains/eq-undefined/not/and/or/nested) run
  then: the ids returned by the compiled SQL equal those selected by filterMatchEntity over the
        same objects, for more than 50 non-empty rounds
  tags: [query, F-4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: Compiled SQL uses the indexes
  given: the package migrations applied to an empty database
  when: EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN runs on compiled type selects, id selects, traversals and deletion checks
  then: each plan SEARCHes the intended index; none SCANs the entity table
  tags: [query, F-4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: One statement per query
  given: a query with a filter, a reference traversal, a union and orderBy + limit
  when: it executes
  then:
    - exactly one SELECT statement reaches SQLite
    - its parameters contain no interpolated values
  tags: [query, F-4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: Read-your-writes
  given: an open database with no explicit flush
  when: an object is added and mutated, then db.query(...).run() is called
  then: the result includes the object with its latest values
  tags: [query, F-4.9]
```

```mdl
scenario T-7: Subscriptions fire only on change
  given: a subscription on Filter.type(Person, { age: Filter.gt(18) })
  when: a matching Person is added, an unrelated Organization is added, then the Person's age drops
        to 10
  then: the subscriber is notified twice, with 1 and then 0 results
  tags: [reactivity, F-5]
```

```mdl
scenario T-8: Persistence across reopen
  given: objects, a relation, a parent/child pair, references, a soft-deleted object, a persisted
         dynamic type and an inline blob written and flushed
  when: the scope closes and the file is reopened without registering the dynamic type
  then: every entity, reference, endpoint, parent, deletion flag and blob reads back identically
  tags: [durability, F-6, F-7]
```

```mdl
scenario T-9: A failed write is retried, not dropped
  given: an open database whose entity table is made unavailable
  when: objects are added and removed, flush rejects, the table is restored and flush runs again
  then: after reopen both the additions and the deletions are present
  tags: [durability, F-6.4]
```

## Rules

```mdl
rule echo-sqlite-no-whole-space-read: Never read or hydrate a whole space
  Flag any code path that reads every entity row of a space into memory or iterates the whole working
  set to answer a request: a SELECT over the entity table without a selective predicate (id, id list,
  compiled query, or kind = type) or a LIMIT; a store method that returns all rows of a space; a loop
  that hydrates each row of such a result; or any query, ref-resolution or subscription path that
  enumerates `#tracked`/the working set to find matches. Loading the persisted type rows on open is
  allowed, as are migrations, garbage collection that deletes by predicate in SQL, and tests.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/**/*.ts
  grep: SELECT|list\(|entities\(\)|values\(\)|forEach|for \(const
  severity: error
  question: Does this change read or hydrate every row of a space, or scan the whole working set, to answer a request?
```

```mdl
rule echo-sqlite-no-js-query-evaluation: Queries are answered by SQL, not by JavaScript matching
  Flag code outside the registry scope that decides query membership, traversal, ordering, limit or
  skip in JavaScript — calls to `filterMatchEntity`, `Filter.toPredicate`, or hand-written predicate
  loops over hydrated objects, `.sort` / `.slice` on query results, or a fallback that evaluates a
  query AST in memory when compilation declines. Hydrating the final rows of a compiled statement is
  fine; so is matching against the in-process registry for `Scope.registry()` queries.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/**/*.ts
  grep: filterMatchEntity|toPredicate|\.sort\(|\.slice\(|\.filter\(
  severity: error
  question: Does this code evaluate query filters, traversal, ordering or paging in JavaScript instead of SQL?
```

```mdl
rule echo-sqlite-bound-parameters: SQL values are bound, never interpolated
  Flag SQL built by string concatenation or template interpolation of values — ids, typenames,
  property values, lists — into `sql.unsafe`, `sql.literal` or a raw string. Values go through the
  `sql` tagged template or the params array, and lists are one JSON-array parameter read with
  `json_each(?)`. JSON paths, CTE names and index names emitted as literals by the compiler are
  allowed when they come from the query AST's property names through the compiler's quoting helper.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/**/*.ts
  grep: unsafe|literal|\$\{
  severity: error
  question: Does this code interpolate a runtime value into SQL text instead of binding it?
```

```mdl
rule echo-sqlite-ddl-in-migrations: Schema lives only in numbered migrations
  Flag CREATE, ALTER or DROP statements in TypeScript outside `src/migrations/` (tests excepted), and
  any edit to an already-committed `src/migrations/*.sql` file. New schema is a new numbered file
  listed in `src/migrations/index.ts`.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/**/*.ts
    - src/migrations/*.sql
  grep: CREATE|ALTER|DROP
  severity: error
```

